## The Hero / Hub / Hygiene balance

**Hero — signature moments, about a fifth.** Hero carries the signature moments, about a fifth of the effort: the public launch of The Stop Line, the Signed by Humans founder moment and the earned-validation push once proof exists.

**Hub — sustained programmes, about three fifths.** Hub carries the sustained programmes, about three fifths of the effort: the proof page, owner message test, partner pack, technical proof stories, senior craft series and employee advocacy.

**Hygiene — always-on maintenance, about a fifth.** Hygiene carries the always-on fifth: public identity cleanup, proof-label maintenance, Conversation Management, Observatory response, approved Q&A (questions and answers) reuse and year-end measurement discipline.

## The Activation Cards

No tactic goes on the calendar until it is a card.

### Public identity and proof-library lock

**Insight.** The Stop Line cannot be credible if buyer-facing records conflict or AI claims are not tied to approved proof.

**Idea.** Clean public identity and lock the proof library before any broad promotion of The Stop Line or Signed by Humans.

**Engagement — the mechanic.** The team reads public AI claims, identity records and proof labels in Month 1 against the approval route before anything is promoted.

**Desired outcome.** The B Team enters launch with fewer public contradictions and a proof base that can support The Stop Line.  ·  **Targets.** Public AI claims, buyer-facing public records and proof-library labels are read in Month 1 before launch assets proceed.

**Signature.** The Stop Line  ·  **KPI.** Percentage of public AI claims linked to approved proof and unresolved public-profile conflicts closed.  ·  **Month.** 1

### The Stop Line proof page

**Insight.** Buyers do not need a bigger AI promise; they need to see what is proved, who owns it and where the system stops.

**Idea.** Create one owned page that introduces The Stop Line and shows each approved claim with proof type, senior owner and stated limit.

**Engagement — the mechanic.** Visitors inspect the proof behind the claim before they are asked to book a meeting or accept the AI story.

**Desired outcome.** The B Team has a public proof base that paid, earned, shared and sales activity can all point to.  ·  **Targets.** Approved claims, proof types, senior owners and stated limits are read in Month 2 before the proof page is cleared for launch.

**Signature.** The Stop Line  ·  **KPI.** Proof-page visits, time on page, qualified meeting clicks and percentage of public AI claims linked to approved proof.  ·  **Month.** 2

### Owner control message test

**Insight.** Elena's preference for control and visibility is a strong hypothesis, but it is not settled buyer truth until owners compare the messages.

**Idea.** Test a control-led message against an AI-capability message, both anchored in The Stop Line.

**Engagement — the mechanic.** Owners compare plain-language options that show visibility, ownership and early risk detection without asking them to become technical buyers.

**Desired outcome.** The B Team learns whether control should lead owner-facing communication before scaling the message.  ·  **Targets.** Owner reactions to control-led and AI-capability messages are read in Month 3 before the founder launch language is finalized.

**Signature.** Signed by Humans  ·  **KPI.** Preference split, comprehension score, trust lift, meeting intent and qualitative reasons for choice.  ·  **Month.** 3

### The Stop Line proof launch

**Insight.** The Stop Line needs a public moment only after proof, ownership and limits are visible.

**Idea.** Launch the proof page with approved press and owned materials that explain proof before promotion.

**Engagement — the mechanic.** Audiences are directed from launch coverage and owned channels to the proof page where claims can be inspected.

**Desired outcome.** The Stop Line becomes the named platform for The B Team's AI reputation story.  ·  **Targets.** Launch materials, proof-page readiness and approved AI claims are read in Month 4 before outreach begins.

**Signature.** The Stop Line  ·  **KPI.** Proof-page visits, earned coverage quality, proof citations and qualified meeting clicks.  ·  **Month.** 4

### Signed by Humans founder launch post

**Insight.** The human moment makes the AI story credible when a senior person visibly checks, owns and signs the work.

**Idea.** Publish a founder-led launch post that explains Signed by Humans as the visible promise behind The Stop Line.

**Engagement — the mechanic.** Readers see the senior responsibility behind the claim and are directed to the owned proof page for evidence.

**Desired outcome.** Founder visibility increases while the public story stays inside the approved proof boundary.  ·  **Targets.** Founder copy, proof links and partner-share language are read in Month 5 before shared and paid amplification.

**Signature.** Signed by Humans  ·  **KPI.** Founder-post reach, engagement quality, proof-page clicks, partner reshares and qualified inbound conversations.  ·  **Month.** 5

### Partner proof pack

**Insight.** Referral partners can carry The Stop Line only if the approved proof is simple, repeatable and safe to share.

**Idea.** Build a short partner pack that explains The Stop Line, Signed by Humans and the approved proof types behind each claim.

**Engagement — the mechanic.** Partners get language they can use without inventing claims or stretching evidence.

**Desired outcome.** Referral conversations become easier, more consistent and less exposed to unapproved AI claims.  ·  **Targets.** Partner language, proof types and approved reference material are read in Month 6 before partner briefings.

**Signature.** Proof before promotion  ·  **KPI.** Partner pack downloads, partner briefings completed, referred qualified leads and claim-compliance checks.  ·  **Month.** 6

### Technical proof story

**Insight.** Technical buyers need proof that AI supports delivery without taking ownership away from senior people.

**Idea.** Publish technical artifacts and explanatory content that show the claim, the proof, the owner and the stop line.

**Engagement — the mechanic.** Technical buyers can inspect the artifact and use it in sales or architecture conversations.

**Desired outcome.** The Stop Line gains technical credibility without claiming autonomous AI.  ·  **Targets.** Technical artifacts, approval checks and proof labels are read in Month 7 before technical content is released.

**Signature.** The Stop Line  ·  **KPI.** Technical artifact views, time on page, sales use and qualified technical conversations.  ·  **Month.** 7

### Senior craft story series

**Insight.** Senior Sofia talent and careful buyers will believe the AI story only if it protects judgment rather than celebrating automation alone.

**Idea.** Publish a recurring series under Signed by Humans that shows how senior people review work, make decisions and define the stop line.

**Engagement — the mechanic.** Readers see craft, judgment and responsibility inside AI-supported delivery.

**Desired outcome.** The talent story and buyer story reinforce each other without turning The B Team into a broad ethics platform.  ·  **Targets.** Craft stories, spokesperson materials and recruitment language are read in Month 8 before the series publishes.

**Signature.** Signed by Humans  ·  **KPI.** Senior-talent engagement, profile visits, qualified candidate interest, buyer engagement and proof-page clicks.  ·  **Month.** 8

### Conversation Management and Observatory baseline

**Insight.** A plan of bursts cannot hold reputation between moments; The Stop Line needs daily listening and disciplined response.

**Idea.** Run the Conversation Management loop and Observatory newsroom with approved answers and escalation triggers.

**Engagement — the mechanic.** The team listens, triages, responds and escalates only within approved proof and stop-line limits.

**Desired outcome.** The Stop Line remains consistent in quiet months and under challenge.  ·  **Targets.** Questions, comments, AI-trust moments and approved answers are read in Month 9 and then daily before response.

**Signature.** The Stop Line  ·  **KPI.** Response time, issue resolution, message consistency, approved-answer reuse and escalation accuracy.  ·  **Month.** 9

### Earned validation outreach

**Insight.** The credibility gap is not reach; it is whether independent voices can see proof worth citing.

**Idea.** Pitch The Stop Line to journalists, analysts and named experts only after proof exists.

**Engagement — the mechanic.** Professional influencers receive a fact sheet, proof page and briefing route that keep the story inside approved evidence.

**Desired outcome.** Third-party validation makes The Stop Line harder to dismiss and gives paid, shared and owned channels stronger proof to carry.  ·  **Targets.** Earned pitch angles, proof citations and briefing materials are read in Month 10 before outreach.

**Signature.** The Stop Line  ·  **KPI.** Briefings secured, coverage quality, proof citations and qualified inbound conversations.  ·  **Month.** 10

### Employee advocacy push

**Insight.** Employees and senior practitioners can make Signed by Humans visible when they repeat the story from lived craft, not slogans.

**Idea.** Equip employees and advocates with approved social copy, craft examples and proof-page links.

**Engagement — the mechanic.** Employees share approved, role-specific posts that point back to proof and senior ownership.

**Desired outcome.** The Stop Line widens through credible employee advocacy without adding unapproved AI claims.  ·  **Targets.** Employee posts, craft examples and proof links are read in Month 11 before advocacy goes live.

**Signature.** Signed by Humans  ·  **KPI.** Employee participation, post quality, engagement quality, proof-page clicks and candidate interest.  ·  **Month.** 11

### Close scorecard and message recalibration

**Insight.** The Stop Line remains durable only if measurement tightens the story instead of widening the claim.

**Idea.** Close the executive scorecard, approve message changes and name next-year proof gaps.

**Engagement — the mechanic.** Leadership reads the year-end proof, trust, lead, partner and talent signals before approving the next cycle.

**Desired outcome.** The B Team knows what The Stop Line proved, what it did not prove and what must be built next.  ·  **Targets.** Scorecard results, message changes and proof gaps are read in Month 12 before the next-year plan is approved.

**Signature.** The Stop Line  ·  **KPI.** Executive scorecard completion, message changes approved, next-year proof gaps named and claim-compliance rate.  ·  **Month.** 12

